+# Nuvra AI Architecture — Phase 2: Schema-Driven Generation
+
+**Author:** Manus AI
+**Date:** Feb 26, 2026
+
+---
+
+## 1. Overview
+
+This document outlines the architecture for Nuvra's real AI generation engine, moving from the Phase 1 mock (which returned raw HTML) to a structured, schema-driven pipeline. This design makes AI generation more reliable, consistent, and extensible, laying the foundation for generating complex web apps, not just static pages.
+
+The core principle is **separation of concerns**: the AI's job is to describe *what* the page should contain (the schema), while Nuvra's renderer decides *how* to build it (the HTML/CSS).
+
+## 2. The Schema-Driven Pipeline
+
+The new generation process follows a clean, three-step pipeline:
+
+| Step | Component | Input | Output | Description |
+| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
+| 1 | **AI Provider** | `(prompt, options)` | `ProviderResult` | The selected AI provider (Mock, OpenAI, etc.) interprets the user's prompt and returns a structured `PageSchema` object. |
+| 2 | **Schema Renderer** | `PageSchema` | `{ html, css }` | The renderer traverses the `PageSchema` and converts each section into its corresponding HTML and CSS using a library of predefined section renderers. |
+| 3 | **GrapesJS** | `{ html, css }` | Rendered Page | The final HTML and CSS are loaded into the GrapesJS editor, presenting the fully built page to the user for editing. |
+
+This entire flow is orchestrated by `aiEngine.js`, which exposes a single public function: `generatePage(prompt, options)`.
+
+```mermaid
+graph TD
+    A[User Prompt] --> B{aiEngine.generatePage};
+    B --> C{1. AI Provider};
+    C -- "Generates" --> D(PageSchema JSON);
+    B --> E{2. Schema Renderer};
+    D --> E;
+    E -- "Renders" --> F({html, css});
+    B --> G[3. GrapesJS Editor];
+    F --> G;
+```
+
+## 3. Core Components
+
+### 3.1. `PageSchema` (`src/ai/pageSchema.js`)
+
+This is the **canonical, intermediate representation** of an AI-generated page. It's a simple JavaScript object that describes the page's structure and content semantically. By forcing the AI to output this structured format instead of raw HTML, we gain immense reliability and control.
+
+- **`name`**: The page title.
+- **`settings`**: Global styles like `theme`, `accentColor`, and `fontFamily`.
+- **`sections`**: An ordered array of `SectionSchema` objects.
+  - **`type`**: The type of section (e.g., `'hero'`, `'features'`). This must match a key in the `SchemaRenderer`'s registry.
+  - **`props`**: A key-value map of data for that section (e.g., `{ title: 'Hello', subtitle: 'World' }`).
+
+This schema is now stored with each page in the project's JSON. This is critical for future features like re-rendering a page with a new theme or exporting to different frameworks (e.g., React, Vue).
+
+### 3.2. AI Providers (`src/ai/mockProvider.js`, `src/ai/aiEngine.js`)
+
+The provider system remains pluggable, but the contract has changed. All providers (including the mock) now return a `ProviderResult` containing a `PageSchema`.
+
+- **`MockProvider`**: The mock provider is now more sophisticated. It uses simple keyword matching to classify the user's prompt and returns a relevant, pre-defined `PageSchema` template. This allows for realistic, context-aware testing without an API key.
+- **Real Providers (OpenAI, Anthropic)**: The system prompt for real LLMs has been completely rewritten. It now instructs the AI to act as a designer and return a `PageSchema` JSON object, providing it with the list of available section types and their expected props. This constrains the LLM to the Nuvra design system, ensuring all generated pages are original and consistent.
+
+### 3.3. `SchemaRenderer` (`src/ai/schemaRenderer.js`)
+
+This new component is the heart of the generation process. It is a **pure function** that converts a `PageSchema` into final HTML and CSS.
+
+- It maintains a registry of **section renderers**, where each key is a section `type` (e.g., `'hero'`) and the value is a function: `(props, settings) => ({ html, css })`.
+- To render a full page, it iterates through the `schema.sections` array, calls the corresponding renderer for each section, and concatenates the HTML and CSS outputs.
+- This design is highly **extensible**: to add a new section type to Nuvra, you only need to add a new entry to the `SECTION_RENDERERS` object. No other code needs to change.
+- It ensures all generated code is **clean, original, and scoped**, as the HTML/CSS templates are defined within Nuvra's codebase, not hallucinated by an LLM.
+
+## 4. Integration with the App
+
+The new pipeline is wired into the main application as follows:
+
+1.  **`app.js`**: The 'Generate' button handler still calls `aiEngine.generatePage(prompt)`.
+2.  The `GenerationResult` returned by the engine now includes the `schema` object alongside the `html` and `css`.
+3.  `app.js` calls `pageManager.addGeneratedPage()`, passing the `html`, `css`, and the new `schema` object.
+4.  **`pageManager.js`**: The `addGeneratedPage` function has been updated to accept and store the `schema` object as part of the page's data. This ensures it's saved in the project's `localStorage` state.
+
+## 5. Design Decisions & Benefits
+
+| Decision | Rationale | Benefit |
+| :--- | :--- | :--- |
+| **Schema-First Generation** | LLMs are better at structured data generation than pixel-perfect HTML/CSS. | **Reliability & Consistency.** Eliminates malformed HTML, broken styles, and inconsistent designs. Output is always valid and parseable. |
+| **Renderer Owns Markup** | The `SchemaRenderer` contains all HTML/CSS templates. | **Originality & Extensibility.** All generated code is original to Nuvra, avoiding legal issues. New components can be added by editing one file. |
+| **Contextual Mock Provider** | The mock provider uses keyword matching to return different schema templates. | **Realistic Offline Development.** Allows for rapid, context-aware testing of the full pipeline without needing API keys or incurring costs. |
+| **Persist the Schema** | The generated `PageSchema` is saved with the page data. | **Future-Proofing.** Enables future features like theme switching (re-rendering the same schema with new settings) or exporting to other frameworks. |
+
+This architecture successfully decouples AI intent from final presentation, creating a robust and scalable foundation for Nuvra's generation capabilities.
