+# Nuvra — Phase 3 Architecture: App Builder Core
+
+**Date**: Feb 26, 2026
+**Author**: Manus AI
+
+## 1. Overview
+
+Phase 3 marks Nuvra's evolution from a static website builder into a true, AI-ready **web application builder**. This phase introduces the foundational primitives required to construct data-driven applications like dashboards, CRUD interfaces, and internal tools. The core principle is a **schema-first architecture**, where every part of the application is defined by serializable, AI-generatable structures.
+
+This document outlines the five core systems introduced in this phase and explains how they work together to create a powerful, extensible, and fully original app-building foundation.
+
+## 2. The Five Core Systems
+
+Nuvra's app-building capabilities are built upon five interconnected, schema-driven systems. These systems are designed to be modular, with clear separation of concerns, allowing for future expansion without requiring a full rewrite.
+
+| System | File(s) | Responsibility |
+| :--- | :--- | :--- |
+| **Data Model** | `data/dataModel.js`, `data/collectionManager.js` | Defines and manages the application's data structures (collections, fields) and provides an in-memory store. |
+| **State Management** | `state/stateManager.js` | Manages reactive state at the app, page, and component levels, enabling dynamic UIs. |
+| **App Components** | `app-components/appComponents.js` | Provides a library of data-aware UI components (forms, tables, lists) that are the building blocks of apps. |
+| **Logic & Actions** | `actions/actionEngine.js` | A lightweight engine for handling user interactions and data operations (e.g., submit, fetch, delete). |
+| **Page Semantics** | `core/pageSemantics.js` | Distinguishes between static "marketing" pages and dynamic "app" pages, applying different layouts and runtimes. |
+
+## 3. System Architecture Deep Dive
+
+### 3.1. Data Model System
+
+The Data Model is the heart of any application. Nuvra's system is designed for simplicity and schema-driven integrity.
+
+-   **Collections & Fields**: Data is organized into **Collections** (e.g., `users`, `products`), which are defined by a `CollectionSchema`. Each schema contains a map of **Fields** (e.g., `name: { type: 'text' }`, `price: { type: 'number' }`). This strict, typed schema ensures data integrity.
+-   **In-Memory `DataStore`**: For Phase 3, all data is held in a simple, in-memory `DataStore`. This store provides synchronous CRUD operations (`create`, `query`, `getById`, `update`, `delete`). It is designed to be easily replaceable with a persistent backend (e.g., Supabase, Firebase) in a future phase.
+-   **`CollectionManager`**: This UI-facing module (`src/data/collectionManager.js`) provides functions for creating, deleting, and managing collections from the sidebar UI. It also includes pre-built templates for common use cases like `Users`, `Tasks`, and `Products`.
+
+```mermaid
+graph TD
+    A[UI: Data Panel] -- Manages --> B(CollectionManager);
+    B -- Creates/Deletes --> C(CollectionSchema);
+    B -- Modifies --> D(DataStore);
+    C -- Defines structure for --> D;
+```
+
+### 3.2. State Management System
+
+To create dynamic UIs, Nuvra needed a reactive state management system. The `StateManager` provides this with a simple, hierarchical model.
+
+-   **State Nodes (`StateNode`)**: The core of the system is the `StateNode`, a reactive key-value store. It implements the Observer pattern, allowing different parts of the UI to `subscribe()` to changes in specific state keys.
+-   **Hierarchical State**: State is organized into three levels:
+    1.  **App State**: A single, global store for cross-page state (e.g., logged-in user, theme).
+    2.  **Page State**: A store for each page, holding page-level variables (e.g., `selectedId` in a master-detail view, search queries, table sort order).
+    3.  **Component State**: Ephemeral state for individual component instances (e.g., `isLoading` for a specific form).
+-   **Data Flow**: The flow is unidirectional. UI events trigger **Actions**, which update the **State**, which in turn causes the UI to re-render specific elements.
+
+### 3.3. App Components
+
+While marketing pages are built with simple blocks, app pages are constructed from intelligent, data-aware **App Components**.
+
+-   **Component Registry**: `appComponents.js` defines a library of components like `data-table`, `data-form`, `data-list`, and `stat-display`. Each component is a function that returns a string of HTML and CSS.
+-   **Data-Binding Attributes**: The key innovation is the use of `data-nv-*` attributes in the component templates. These attributes are declarative instructions for the `AppRuntime`.
+    -   `data-nv-component="data-table"`: Identifies the component type.
+    -   `data-nv-collection="users"`: Binds the component to a data collection.
+    -   `data-nv-bind="page.selectedUser.name"`: Binds an element's `textContent` to a state variable.
+    -   `data-nv-action="submit"`: Attaches a click handler that triggers a specific action.
+    -   `data-nv-show-when="app.user.isAdmin"`: Conditionally shows/hides an element.
+
+### 3.4. Logic & Actions Engine
+
+The `ActionEngine` is a lightweight system that connects UI events to data operations.
+
+-   **Action Registry**: The engine maintains a map of action handlers (e.g., `submit`, `delete`, `fetch`, `select`).
+-   **Event Handling**: The `AppRuntime` finds all `[data-nv-action]` elements and attaches a generic click listener. When clicked, this listener calls `ActionEngine.run()`, passing the action ID and a context object (containing the element, page ID, and any configuration).
+-   **Decoupled Logic**: This design decouples the component's HTML from the business logic. A button only needs to declare `data-nv-action="delete"`; it doesn't need to know *how* the deletion is performed.
+
+### 3.5. Page Semantics & The App Runtime
+
+This system ties everything together by distinguishing between page types.
+
+-   **Page Kinds (`marketing` vs. `app`)**: Every page now has a `pageConfig` that defines its `type` (e.g., `landing`, `dashboard`). Each type belongs to a `kind`. This allows Nuvra to treat them differently.
+-   **Conditional Runtime (`AppRuntime`)**: The new `AppRuntime` (`src/runtime/appRuntime.js`) is the engine that brings app pages to life. It is **only mounted for `app` pages**.
+    -   When an app page is loaded, the runtime scans the DOM for `data-nv-*` attributes.
+    -   It hydrates the static HTML, attaching state subscriptions and action handlers.
+    -   For example, it finds a `[data-nv-bind="page.recordCount"]` span and subscribes it to the `recordCount` key in the page's state node. When the state changes, only that span's text is updated.
+
+This approach avoids the overhead of a virtual DOM while providing the necessary reactivity for building complex applications.
+
+## 4. Conclusion & Next Steps
+
+Phase 3 successfully establishes a robust, schema-driven foundation for building web applications within Nuvra. The architecture is designed for extensibility, originality, and future AI integration.
+
+With this foundation in place, the immediate next step (Phase 4) will be to teach the AI how to generate these new app structures. The AI will learn to:
+
+1.  Generate `CollectionSchema` definitions from a prompt.
+2.  Generate `PageSchema` objects that use the new `app` components.
+3.  Compose `data-nv-*` attributes to wire up data, state, and actions correctly.
+
+This will unlock Nuvra's ability to generate not just static websites, but fully functional, data-driven web applications from a single natural language prompt.
