+# Nuvra Architecture — Phase 5: Publish & Hosting Foundation
+
+**Author**: Manus AI
+**Date**: Feb 26, 2026
+
+## 1. Overview
+
+Phase 5 introduces the **Publish & Hosting Foundation**, a system that closes the loop between building, previewing, and deploying a Nuvra project. It takes the high-fidelity execution model established in Phase 4 and uses it to produce a production-ready, deployable static site bundle.
+
+The core principle of this phase is **Deployment Parity**: the published output is guaranteed to be functionally identical to what the user sees in Preview Mode. This eliminates surprises and ensures that a project that works in preview will work in production.
+
+## 2. Key Goals & Requirements
+
+-   **One-Click Publish**: The user can initiate the entire publish process with a single click.
+-   **Production-Ready Output**: The system must generate a bundle that can be deployed to any standard static hosting provider (e.g., GitHub Pages, Netlify, Vercel) without any further build steps.
+-   **Preview Parity**: The published site must use the exact same rendering and execution path as the Preview Mode to ensure identical behavior.
+-   **Local & Secure**: The entire build process must happen locally in the user's browser. No data, code, or assets are sent to a server.
+-   **User-Friendly Feedback**: The user must be kept informed throughout the process with clear progress indicators and a final build report.
+
+## 3. Core Architectural Components
+
+This phase introduces a new `publish` module that orchestrates the build and packaging process. It sits on top of the existing `preview` module.
+
+| Component | Location | Responsibility |
+| :--- | :--- | :--- |
+| **`PublishManager`** | `src/publish/publishManager.js` | Orchestrates the entire end-to-end publish flow. It manages the UI (publish modal, progress bar, build report), runs the `SiteBuilder`, and triggers the final ZIP download. |
+| **`SiteBuilder`** | `src/publish/siteBuilder.js` | The core build engine. It iterates through every page in the project, uses the `PreviewRenderer` to generate the final HTML for each, and assembles a complete manifest of all site files. |
+
+### 3.1. Architectural Flow
+
+The process of publishing a site follows these steps:
+
+1.  **User Action**: The user clicks the "Publish" button in the top bar.
+2.  **`PublishManager` → `openPublishModal()`**: The `PublishManager` is invoked.
+3.  **Build Summary**: It calls `getBuildSummary()` from the `SiteBuilder` to get a list of all pages that will be included in the build.
+4.  **Show Modal**: It displays a publish modal, showing the user the list of pages, the number of app vs. marketing pages, and an input for the site name.
+5.  **User Confirmation**: The user confirms the build by clicking "Build & Download ZIP".
+6.  **`SiteBuilder` → `buildSite()`**: The `PublishManager` calls the `SiteBuilder` to begin the production build.
+7.  **Iterative Rendering**: The `SiteBuilder` loops through every page in the project.
+    -   For each page, it calls `buildPreviewDocument()`—the **exact same function used by Preview Mode**—to generate the final, self-contained HTML string.
+    -   It adds the resulting HTML to an in-memory list of `SiteFile` objects.
+8.  **Manifest Generation**: After all pages are rendered, the `SiteBuilder` creates a `manifest.json` and a `README.md` file containing build metadata.
+9.  **ZIP Assembly**: The `PublishManager` receives the complete list of `SiteFile` objects from the `SiteBuilder`.
+    -   It dynamically loads `fflate`, a lightweight, pure-JS ZIP library, from a CDN.
+    -   It uses `fflate` to assemble a `.zip` file from the file list entirely in the browser's memory.
+10. **Download**: The `PublishManager` creates a `Blob` from the generated ZIP data and triggers a browser download.
+11. **Build Report**: Finally, it displays a build report modal, showing the user whether the build was successful, listing any warnings (e.g., for pages that failed to render), and providing quick-start deployment instructions.
+
+## 4. Design Decisions & Rationale
+
+| Decision | Rationale |
+| :--- | :--- |
+| **Reuse `PreviewRenderer`** | This is the most critical decision of this phase. By using the exact same `buildPreviewDocument` function for both preview and publish, we **guarantee** that the output will be identical. There is no separate "production" render path that could diverge and cause bugs. |
+| **In-Memory Build** | The entire build process, from rendering HTML to assembling the ZIP file, happens in the browser's memory. No files are written to the local filesystem, and no server is required. This makes the process extremely fast, secure, and portable. |
+| **Lazy-Loaded ZIP Library** | The `fflate` library is only loaded on-demand when the user clicks the publish button. This keeps the initial application payload small and light, as users who don't publish never download the ZIP code. |
+| **Decoupled `SiteBuilder` and `PublishManager`** | The `SiteBuilder` is responsible only for generating the file manifest. The `PublishManager` is responsible for the UI and the final packaging (ZIP). This separation of concerns makes the system more modular and testable. We could easily swap out the ZIP packager for a different deployment method (e.g., a direct push to a cloud provider) in the future without changing the core `SiteBuilder`. |
+| **Static `index.html`** | By convention, the page with the ID `home` (or the first page in the list if `home` doesn't exist) is always named `index.html`. This ensures that the generated site will work out-of-the-box on any static hosting provider. |
+
+## 5. Impact & Future-Proofing
+
+This phase delivers the final piece of the core local-first Nuvra experience. The user can now go from a blank canvas to a fully built, data-driven application and a deployable ZIP file without any external dependencies.
+
+This foundation enables several future paths:
+
+-   **Cloud Hosting**: The `PublishManager` can be extended to include a "Publish to Nuvra Cloud" option. Instead of creating a ZIP, it would upload the `SiteBundle` to a serverless function that deploys the files to a service like AWS S3 or Cloudflare Pages.
+-   **CI/CD Integration**: The `manifest.json` file provides a hook for external tooling. A CI/CD pipeline could be configured to automatically build and deploy a Nuvra project whenever the project's JSON file is updated in a Git repository.
+-   **Mobile Builds (Capacitor/TWA)**: The generated static bundle is the perfect input for tools like Capacitor or Trusted Web Activities (TWA), which wrap web content into native mobile applications. A future phase could add a "Build for Mobile" option that takes the site bundle and runs it through the appropriate command-line tools.
