+# Nuvra — AI Website & App Builder
+
+[![Status](https://img.shields.io/badge/status-in_development-orange.svg)](https://github.com/stablin09-rgb/nuvra.github.io)
+
+**Nuvra** is an open-source, AI-powered builder designed to create beautiful websites, web applications, dashboards, and internal tools. It combines a visual drag-and-drop editor with a powerful, pluggable AI engine to go from a simple prompt to a fully functional page in seconds.
+
+This project is an original work and is not a clone of other AI builders. It is built with extensibility and originality as its core principles.
+
+---
+
+## ✨ Features
+
+- **Visual Drag & Drop Editor:** Built on the powerful and flexible [GrapesJS](https://grapesjs.com/) framework.
+- **Pluggable AI Engine:** Switch between different LLM providers (OpenAI, Anthropic) or use the built-in mock provider for offline development. The architecture is designed to be easily extended with new models.
+- **Multi-Page Support:** Create, rename, and delete pages within a single project.
+- **Component Library:** A rich library of pre-designed blocks for layout, content, marketing, forms, and data visualization.
+- **Full Project Export/Import:** Save and load entire projects as a single JSON file for easy backup and transfer.
+- **Production-Ready Exports:** Export single pages or the entire site as clean, standalone HTML files.
+- **Clean, Modern UI:** A minimalist and intuitive interface that puts the focus on building.
+
+## 🚀 Getting Started
+
+1.  **Clone the repository:**
+    ```bash
+    git clone https://github.com/stablin09-rgb/nuvra.github.io.git
+    ```
+2.  **Navigate to the directory:**
+    ```bash
+    cd nuvra.github.io
+    ```
+3.  **Serve `index.html`:**
+    You can use any simple web server. For example, with Python:
+    ```bash
+    python3 -m http.server
+    ```
+    Or with Node.js (using `serve`):
+    ```bash
+    npx serve
+    ```
+4.  Open your browser to the provided local URL (e.g., `http://localhost:8000`).
+
+## 🏗️ Architecture Overview
+
+The Nuvra frontend is built with vanilla JavaScript (ESM) and follows a modular, separation-of-concerns pattern. All source code is located in the `/src` directory.
+
+- `/src/core`: Main application logic, including the entry point (`app.js`), page management (`pageManager.js`), and local storage persistence (`storage.js`).
+- `/src/ai`: The provider-agnostic AI generation layer (`aiEngine.js`) and its configuration UI (`aiSettings.js`).
+- `/src/blocks`: The library of all drag-and-drop components (`blockLibrary.js`).
+- `/src/ui`: The main stylesheet (`styles.css`) and other UI-related code.
+- `/src/utils`: Reusable helper functions (modals, toasts, etc.).
+
+For a detailed breakdown of the long-term vision, component architecture, and proposed hosting infrastructure, please see [ARCHITECTURE.md](ARCHITECTURE.md).
+
+## 🔧 Using the AI Engine
+
+By default, Nuvra uses a **mock AI provider** that generates a template page without needing an API key. This is great for offline development and testing the UI.
+
+To use a real LLM for generation:
+
+1.  Click the **"AI Settings"** button in the top bar.
+2.  Select your desired provider (e.g., OpenAI).
+3.  Enter your API key. **Your key is stored exclusively in your browser's `localStorage` and is never sent to any server.**
+4.  (Optional) Specify a model name to override the default (e.g., `gpt-4o-mini`).
+5.  Click **"Save & Apply"**.
+
+Now, when you use the AI prompt bar, Nuvra will send requests to the configured provider.
+
+## 🤝 Contributing
+
+This project is actively being developed. Contributions, feedback, and bug reports are welcome. Please feel free to open an issue or submit a pull request.
+
